# Comment remediation sweep

Read when comment-remediated.sh reports `comment remediation required` for a file you are
editing. Sweep the **whole file**, not the diff. English here for the same reason the script
speaks English: this is loaded on demand during work, and English is the cheaper encoding.

## Before judging a borderline case

The authority is the **Comment Rules** section of `docs/rules.md`. If it is already in your
context, do not re-read it — what follows is that section distilled to what a sweep decides.
If it is not, and a case below does not settle the question, read that section only.

## Delete

- **How / implementation means.** The code conveys it.
- **Where it is called from.** Registration and call-site notes coupled to organization.
- **Change history / 経緯.** Migration story, incident backstory, "why we switched". It rots;
  it belongs in the PR and commit log.
- **Restatement.** A comment that repeats the name or the signature. Tautologies.
- **Meta-argument.** Prose arguing why the surrounding rule is worth having.
- **Boilerplate character.** Anything resting on a premise that lapses the first time this
  repository is used as a template: "you have just forked this", "the owner is a placeholder",
  its current positioning, its distribution form, "we are still in PoC". The procedure itself
  belongs in `docs/get-started/**`, which is discarded when the premise dies.

## Keep

- **The contract.** For an exported declaration, a `Name`-prefixed sentence stating what it
  does and the meaning of its inputs, outputs and errors. Necessary and sufficient.
- **A constraint whose premise sits right here.** Test: could someone make this statement
  false *without editing this declaration*? If not, keep it — it cannot go stale unseen
  ("do not extract this helper, it shifts the skip count"; "these two calls must not be
  reordered"). If they could — upstream behavior, an operational policy, a business rule —
  it has no home here.
- **A dependency's specific behavior** observed at the call site. That is a property of the
  thing being called, not a decision, and this is its home.

## Route, do not dump

A choice among alternatives, a rejected option, a threat model, an architecture-wide policy →
`docs/adr/`. Business meaning → `docs/spec/**`. Layer or package convention → that package's
`README.md`. Keep only the operative residue plus a link. If a candidate fits no destination,
the code was the right place — leave it.

## While rewriting

- **Correct outranks everything.** A comment that has drifted from the behavior is the worst
  case in the file. Verify each claim against the code before keeping it.
- **Never invent a Why.** If you cannot derive it from the code, a document or the config,
  leave it out and raise the gap instead.
- **Keep each comment in the language it is already written in.** Do not translate.
- **Idiomatic code needs no comment.** Volume is itself a cost — a file where everything is
  explained can no longer show which comment is load-bearing.
- Applies to every language, not only Go. Non-Go is higher risk: `revive` does not reach it.

## Finish

Append to `comment-remediated.toml`, in this directory:

```toml
"<repository-relative path>" = "<YYYY-MM-DD>"
```

Record the file only once its whole comment stock has been reviewed. An entry that claims a
sweep that did not happen is worse than no entry — nothing will look at that file again.
